{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000013_3","statement":"Myelodysplastic/myeloproliferative neoplasms are diseases in which the bone marrow makes too many white blood cells.","source_span":"the bone marrow makes too many white blood cells"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000013_3","statement":"Myelodysplastic/myeloproliferative neoplasms have features of both myelodysplastic syndromes and myeloproliferative neoplasms.","source_span":"have features of both myelodysplastic syndromes and myeloproliferative neoplasms"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000013_3","statement":"The 3 main types of myelodysplastic/myeloproliferative neoplasms are chronic myelomonocytic leukemia (CMML), juvenile myelomonocytic leukemia (JMML), and atypical chronic myelogenous leukemia (CML).","source_span":"Chronic myelomonocytic leukemia (CMML).    -  Juvenile myelomonocytic leukemia (JMML).    - Atypical chronic myelogenous leukemia (CML)"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000013_3","statement":"When a myelodysplastic/myeloproliferative neoplasm does not match any of the 3 main types, it is called MDS/MPN, unclassifiable (MDS/MPN-UC).","source_span":"it is called myelodysplastic/myeloproliferative neoplasm, unclassifiable (MDS/MPN-UC)"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000013_3","statement":"Myelodysplastic/myeloproliferative neoplasms may progress to acute leukemia.","source_span":"Myelodysplastic/myeloproliferative neoplasms may progress to acute leukemia."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000013_3","statement":"There is no standard staging system for myelodysplastic/myeloproliferative neoplasms.","source_span":"There is no standard staging system for myelodysplastic/myeloproliferative neoplasms."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000013_3","statement":"Five types of standard treatment for myelodysplastic/myeloproliferative neoplasms are chemotherapy, other drug therapy, stem cell transplant, supportive care, and targeted therapy.","source_span":"Chemotherapy      - Other drug therapy     - Stem cell transplant     -  Supportive care      - Targeted therapy"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000013_3","statement":"The cancer cells in myelodysplastic/myeloproliferative neoplasms do not contain the Philadelphia chromosome that is present in chronic myelogenous leukemia.","source_span":"do not contain the Philadelphia chromosome that is present in chronic myelogenous leukemia"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000013_3","statement":"Bone marrow aspiration and biopsy involves removing a small piece of bone and bone marrow by inserting a needle into the hipbone or breastbone.","source_span":"The removal of a small piece of bone and bone marrow by inserting a needle into the hipbone or breastbone."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000013_3","fact_refs":[0,1],"question":"What are myelodysplastic/myeloproliferative neoplasms?","answer":"They are a group of blood and bone marrow diseases in which the bone marrow makes too many white blood cells, combining features of both myelodysplastic syndromes and myeloproliferative neoplasms."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000013_3","fact_refs":[2],"question":"What are the three main types of myelodysplastic/myeloproliferative neoplasms?","answer":"Chronic myelomonocytic leukemia (CMML), juvenile myelomonocytic leukemia (JMML), and atypical chronic myelogenous leukemia (CML)."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000013_3","fact_refs":[3],"question":"What is it called when an MDS/MPN neoplasm doesn't fit any of the three main types?","answer":"It is called myelodysplastic/myeloproliferative neoplasm, unclassifiable (MDS/MPN-UC)."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000013_3","fact_refs":[4],"question":"What can myelodysplastic/myeloproliferative neoplasms progress to?","answer":"They may progress to acute leukemia."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000013_3","fact_refs":[5],"question":"What staging system is used for myelodysplastic/myeloproliferative neoplasms?","answer":"There is no standard staging system; treatment is instead based on the specific type of the neoplasm the patient has."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000013_3","fact_refs":[6],"question":"What are the five standard treatment types for myelodysplastic/myeloproliferative neoplasms?","answer":"Chemotherapy, other drug therapy, stem cell transplant, supportive care, and targeted therapy."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000013_3","fact_refs":[7],"question":"What genetic marker distinguishes MDS/MPN cancer cells from chronic myelogenous leukemia cells?","answer":"MDS/MPN cancer cells do not contain the Philadelphia chromosome, which is present in chronic myelogenous leukemia."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000013_3_1","statement":"Chronic myelomonocytic leukemia (CMML) is a disease in which too many myelocytes and monocytes (immature white blood cells) are made in the bone marrow.","source_span":"too many myelocytes and monocytes (immature white blood cells) are made in the bone marrow"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000013_3_1","statement":"Older age and being male increase the risk of chronic myelomonocytic leukemia.","source_span":"Older age and being male increase the risk of chronic myelomonocytic leukemia."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000013_3_1","statement":"Possible risk factors for CMML include older age, being male, being exposed to certain substances at work or in the environment, being exposed to radiation, and past treatment with certain anticancer drugs.","source_span":"Being exposed to certain substances at work or in the environment.    - Being exposed to radiation.    - Past treatment with certain anticancer drugs."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000013_3_1","statement":"Signs and symptoms of chronic myelomonocytic leukemia include fever, weight loss, and feeling very tired.","source_span":"Signs and symptoms of chronic myelomonocytic leukemia include fever, weight loss, and feeling very tired."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000013_3_1","statement":"Prognosis and treatment options for CMML depend on white blood cell or platelet counts, whether the patient is anemic, the amount of blasts, the amount of hemoglobin, and whether there are certain chromosome changes.","source_span":"The prognosis (chance of recovery) and treatment options for CMML depend on the following:         - The number of white blood cells or platelets in the blood or bone marrow.    - Whether the patient is anemic.    - The amount of blasts in the blood or bone marrow.    - The amount of hemoglobin in red blood cells.    - Whether there are certain changes in the chromosomes."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000013_3_1","statement":"Treatment of CMML may include chemotherapy with one or more agents, stem cell transplant, or a clinical trial of a new treatment.","source_span":"Treatment of chronic myelomonocytic leukemia (CMML) may include the following:         -  Chemotherapy with one or more agents.    -  Stem cell transplant.    - A clinical trial of a new treatment."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000013_3_1","fact_refs":[0],"question":"What happens in chronic myelomonocytic leukemia (CMML)?","answer":"Too many myelocytes and monocytes, which are immature white blood cells, are made in the bone marrow, eventually crowding out red blood cells and platelets and causing infection, anemia, or easy bleeding."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000013_3_1","fact_refs":[1,2],"question":"What are the risk factors for chronic myelomonocytic leukemia?","answer":"Older age, being male, exposure to certain substances at work or in the environment, exposure to radiation, and past treatment with certain anticancer drugs."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000013_3_1","fact_refs":[3],"question":"What symptoms are associated with CMML?","answer":"Fever, weight loss, and feeling very tired, along with possible infection, easy bruising or bleeding, and pain or fullness below the ribs."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000013_3_1","fact_refs":[4],"question":"What factors affect prognosis for CMML patients?","answer":"White blood cell or platelet counts, whether the patient is anemic, the amount of blasts, the amount of hemoglobin, and certain chromosome changes."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000013_3_1","fact_refs":[5],"question":"What treatments are used for chronic myelomonocytic leukemia?","answer":"Chemotherapy with one or more agents, stem cell transplant, or a clinical trial of a new treatment."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000013_3_1","fact_refs":[1],"question":"Which two demographic factors increase the risk of CMML?","answer":"Older age and being male."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000013_3_2","statement":"Juvenile myelomonocytic leukemia (JMML) is a childhood disease in which too many myelocytes and monocytes (immature white blood cells) are made in the bone marrow.","source_span":"Juvenile myelomonocytic leukemia is a childhood disease in which too many myelocytes and monocytes (immature white blood cells) are made in the bone marrow."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000013_3_2","statement":"JMML is a rare childhood cancer that occurs more often in children younger than 2 years.","source_span":"occurs more often in children younger than 2 years"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000013_3_2","statement":"Children who have neurofibromatosis type 1 and males have an increased risk of juvenile myelomonocytic leukemia.","source_span":"Children who have neurofibromatosis type 1 and males have an increased risk of juvenile myelomonocytic leukemia."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000013_3_2","statement":"Signs and symptoms of JMML include fever, weight loss, and feeling very tired.","source_span":"Signs and symptoms of juvenile myelomonocytic leukemia include fever, weight loss, and feeling very tired."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000013_3_2","statement":"Other JMML symptoms can include infections such as bronchitis or tonsillitis, easy bruising or bleeding, skin rash, and painless swelling of lymph nodes in the neck, underarm, stomach, or groin.","source_span":"Painless swelling of the lymph nodes in the neck, underarm, stomach, or groin."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000013_3_2","statement":"Prognosis and treatment options for JMML depend on the age of the child at diagnosis, the number of platelets in the blood, and the amount of a certain type of hemoglobin in red blood cells.","source_span":"The prognosis (chance of recovery) and treatment options for JMML depend on the following:         - The age of the child at diagnosis.    - The number of platelets in the blood.    - The amount of a certain type of hemoglobin in red blood cells."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000013_3_2","statement":"Treatment of JMML may include combination chemotherapy, stem cell transplant, 13-cis-retinoic acid therapy, or a clinical trial such as targeted therapy.","source_span":"Combination chemotherapy.    -  Stem cell transplant.    -  13-cis-retinoic acid therapy.    - A clinical trial of a new treatment, such as targeted therapy."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000013_3_2","fact_refs":[0,1],"question":"What is juvenile myelomonocytic leukemia and which age group does it typically affect?","answer":"It is a rare childhood disease in which too many myelocytes and monocytes are made in the bone marrow, and it occurs more often in children younger than 2 years old."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000013_3_2","fact_refs":[2],"question":"What increases a child's risk of developing JMML?","answer":"Having neurofibromatosis type 1 and being male increase the risk of juvenile myelomonocytic leukemia."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000013_3_2","fact_refs":[3,4],"question":"What symptoms may indicate JMML in a child?","answer":"Fever, weight loss, and fatigue, along with infections like bronchitis or tonsillitis, easy bruising or bleeding, skin rash, and painless swelling of lymph nodes in the neck, underarm, stomach, or groin."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000013_3_2","fact_refs":[5],"question":"What three factors affect prognosis and treatment options for JMML?","answer":"The child's age at diagnosis, the number of platelets in the blood, and the amount of a certain type of hemoglobin in red blood cells."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000013_3_2","fact_refs":[6],"question":"What treatment options exist for juvenile myelomonocytic leukemia?","answer":"Combination chemotherapy, stem cell transplant, 13-cis-retinoic acid therapy, or a clinical trial of a new treatment such as targeted therapy."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000013_3_2","fact_refs":[1],"question":"At what age does juvenile myelomonocytic leukemia most commonly occur?","answer":"It occurs more often in children younger than 2 years old."}
